1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:26,000
In the second place, an R sits on an R, so that place is crossed out.

2
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:56,000
Slide on, and cross out every clash.

3
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:26,000
Of these sixteen places, nine are ruled out, without any machine at all.

4
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:56,000
A crib in the right place still doesn't give you the key. For that, Turing designed the bombe.

5
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:26,000
Line the crib up with the ciphertext, and each pair of letters is a link.

6
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:56,000
Draw all the links, and you get a menu. Where links close into loops, the menu gets powerful.

7
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:26,000
At each rotor position, the bombe guesses one letter's plug partner and follows the consequences.

8
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:56,000
A contradiction means that position is impossible, and the drums turn on.

9
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:26,000
Gordon Welchman added the diagonal board, which uses the rule that if A is plugged to B, B is plugged to A.

10
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:56,000
We ran our menu through every position. Without the diagonal board, it stopped six hundred and sixty-two times.

11
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:26,000
With it, it stopped just twice. One stop was false. The other was right, and showed the letter E plugged to J.

12
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:56,000
The first bombe, Victory, arrived in March nineteen forty, built by Harold Keen's engineers.

13
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:26,000
By nineteen forty-five there were about two hundred, run by some two thousand Wrens of the Royal Navy.

14
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:56,000
The hardest Enigma was the navy's, with three rotors from eight and stricter procedures.

15
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:26,000
Turing took it on, he said, because no one else was doing anything about it, and he could have it to himself.

16
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:56,000
It also took captured papers. In May nineteen forty-one, sailors seized codebooks and keys from the submarine U one-ten.

17
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:26,000
Through late nineteen forty-one, decrypts helped steer convoys around the waiting U-boats.

18
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:56,000
Then, on the first of February nineteen forty-two, the U-boats switched to a new Enigma with four rotors.

19
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:26,000
For ten months, Bletchley couldn't read them, and the wolf packs ravaged convoys.

20
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:56,000
In October nineteen forty-two, off Port Said, sailors from H M S Petard climbed into the sinking U five-five-nine for its papers.
